# Why does nobody pay for the pollution?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What is an externality, positive and negative? Why does the market produce too much smoke and too few vaccines?"
next: {"title":"Can a tax fix pollution?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/43-can-a-tax-fix-pollution.cast"}

## Five trillion with no price
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Fuel subsidies
      - Unpaid damage
    values:
      - 1.3
      - 5
    value_labels: true
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 7
    y_label: $ trillion, 2022
    icons:
      - gas pump
      - factory
    gap: 0.6
    label_size: 24
sources:
    - id: imf2023
      title: 'IMF Fossil Fuel Subsidies Data: 2023 Update'
      authors: Simon Black, Antung A. Liu, Ian Parry, Nate Vernon
      year: 2023
      finding: 'Global fossil-fuel subsidies were $7 trillion in 2022 (7.1% of world GDP): $1.3 trillion explicit; consumers did not pay for over $5 trillion of environmental costs, mostly local air pollution and global warming.'
      url: https://www.imf.org/en/Publications/WP/Issues/2023/08/22/IMF-Fossil-Fuel-Subsidies-Data-2023-Update-537281

    text pct "≈ 5% of world output" #b5482e cites ["imf2023"] x 640 y 600 font_size 36 hidden true
    box owner "Boat owner" x 150 y 370 width 230 height 170 icon sailboat font_size 34 hidden true
    box yard "Boatyard" x 500 y 370 width 230 height 170 icon hammer font_size 34 hidden true
    box bakery "Mia's bakery" x 850 y 370 width 230 height 170 icon bread font_size 34 hidden true
    box shot "Flu shot" x 320 y 130 width 230 height 170 icon syringe font_size 34 hidden true
    box nbrs "Neighbours" x 680 y 130 width 230 height 170 icon family font_size 34 hidden true
    edge pay owner -> yard hidden true
    text pay_lab "pays $" x 325 y 420 font_size 30 hidden true
    mark ring around owner yard kind box hidden true
    edge fumes yard -> bakery #b5482e hidden true
    label fumes_lab "fumes" above #b5482e attach_to fumes font_size 30 hidden true
    text ext_lab "externality" #b5482e x 870 y 245 font_size 34 hidden true
    edge good shot -> nbrs #87a878 hidden true
    label good_lab "positive" above #5d8a4e attach_to good font_size 30 hidden true
    text noprice "a price on every cost?" #b5482e x 830 y 520 font_size 36 hidden true

    card title "Five trillion with no price"

Why does nobody pay for the pollution? Start with one bill the whole world ran up in 2022.
    draw axes

Governmen (@draw bar_1@)ts spent about one point three trillion dollars that year keeping fossil fuels cheap.

B: That's the bill someone sees. Now the damage from burning them, the dirty air and the warming, that nobody paid for at all.
    highlight bar_1

    ask "How big was the unpaid damage in 2022? Drag the second bar to your guess." on bar_2 store g default "2" voice b
        right: Close: you said {g} trillion; the IMF's estimate is over {g.true} trillion.
        wrong: You said {g} trillion; the IMF's estimate is over {g.true}. Most people guess far lower.

More than five trillion dollars, by the IMF's estimate: about fiv (@draw pct@)e percent of everything the world produced.

B: Five trillion of cost, and no price tag on any of it. How does a market let that happen?
    highlight bar_2 effect pulse

    erase axes bar_1 bar_2 pct

Shrink it to on (@draw owner yard pay pay_lab@)e town. Harbourtown's boatyard repaints a hull, and the boat's own (@draw ring@)er pays for the job.

The f (@draw bakery fumes fumes_lab@)umes drift over Mia's bakery next door. She isn't part of the deal, and nobody asks her price.

That's an externality (@draw ext_lab@): a cost or a benefit that lands on so (@highlight fumes bakery@)meone outside the trade, with no price attached.

B: And it can be good news. A flu s (@draw shot good nbrs good_lab@)hot protects the person who gets it, and everyone they would have infected.

The proof that markets wa (@draw noprice@)ste nothing needed a price on every cost. Smo (@highlight fumes@)ke has none.

So the real question: when a cost has no price, which way does the market go wrong, and how far?
    highlight fumes good

    pause 0.5

## Too much smoke
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Hulls repainted a year (Q)
    y_label: $ per hull (P)
    demand:
      label: D
    supply:
      label: 'S: yard''s cost'
    equilibrium:
      q_label: '50'
      p_label: $1,000
      shift_q_label: '40'
      shift_p_label: ''
    supply_shift:
      amount: 0
      label: True cost
      arrow: vertical
    region_labels: false
    units:
      price: [0, 2000]
      quantity: [0, 102]
      price_unit: $
      quantity_unit: hulls

    region waste x_from.ref shift_equilibrium_point x_to.ref equilibrium_point between ["supply_shift_curve","demand_curve"] style.fill #c96567 hidden true
    text wastelbl "waste" #b5482e at.data [45,77] font_size 30 hidden true
    text harm "+ $350 harm per hull" #b5482e at.data [74,14] font_size 28 hidden true
    text toomuch "too much" #b5482e at.data [61,6] font_size 30 hidden true
    arrow tm_arrow #b5482e from.data [40,6] to.data [50,6] hidden true
    text illus "illustrative" #8f887c x 860 y 640 font_size 24 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"aftermath"}] then aftermath hidden true
    scratch math work [{"tex":"\\text{true cost} = MC + \\text{harm}"},"market stops at: value = MC","best stops at: value = MC + harm","waste = ½ × $350 × 10 = $1,750 a year"] x 500 y 330 font_size 44 hidden true

    card title "Too much smoke"

A cost outside the deal: now watch what it does to how much gets made. The boatyard's market, wi (@draw illus@)th illustrative numbers.
    draw axes

Demand (@draw demand_curve label_D@) is what boat owners will pay for a repaint. Suppl (@draw supply_curve label_S@)y is what the yard spends on paint, labour and the shed.

They m (@draw equilibrium_point label_E guide_lines label_Qstar label_Pstar@)eet at fifty hulls a year, at a thousand dollars each.

B: Now add Mia. Say each hul (@draw harm@)l's fumes cost the neighbours three hundred and fifty dollars, in ruined bread and doctor's visits.

    quiz "Where does the town's true cost of a hull sit?" voice b
        right: $350 above supply, at every hull: each hull's fumes do the same damage, so the whole curve rises.
        wrong: Each hull's fumes cost the neighbours the same: here, $350 above supply at every hull.
        * On the yard's supply curve
        + $350 above it, at every hull
        * $350 above it, only at the last hull

    draw supply_shift_curve label_S_shift

    move label_S_shift by [-12,14] duration 0.01

Three hundred and fifty dollars above the yard's own cost, at every hull. That's the true cost to the town.
    animate supply_shift.amount -19.6 duration 3

    draw supply_shift_arrow

    erase harm

True cost (@draw shift_equilibrium_point label_E_shift shift_guide_lines label_Q_shift@) meets demand at forty hulls: the number that's best for Harbourtown as a whole.

B: But the yard pain (@draw tm_arrow toomuch@)ts fifty. What's wrong with the last ten?

Each is wor (@draw waste@)th less to its buyer than it costs everyone together. The  (@point at.ref waste gesture circle@)owner gains a little, Mia loses more.

That triang (@draw wastelbl@)le is the waste. Nobody planned it; it's what free ch (@highlight waste effect pulse@)oices add up to when one cost has no price.

B: So the market isn't wrong at random. A cost nobody pays always pushes it the same way: too much.
    highlight toomuch

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b
        intro: Want the numbers behind the triangle?

@math

    erase mathq

    fade axes illus demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point label_E guide_lines label_Qstar label_Pstar supply_shift_curve label_S_shift supply_shift_arrow shift_equilibrium_point label_E_shift shift_guide_lines label_Q_shift waste wastelbl tm_arrow toomuch to 0.12

Write the true cost of a hull as the yard's marginal cost plus the harm to others.
    draw math_box math_line_1

The ma (@draw math_line_2@)rket stops where a buyer's value equals marginal cost; the tow (@draw math_line_3@)n's best stops where it equals cost plus harm.

So the waste is (@draw math_line_4@) half the harm times the extra hulls: half of three hundred and fifty, times ten, is one thousand seven hundred and fifty dollars a year.

    pause 0.5

    erase math

@aftermath

    erase mathq

    fade axes illus demand_curve label_D supply_curve label_S equilibrium_point label_E guide_lines label_Qstar label_Pstar supply_shift_curve label_S_shift supply_shift_arrow shift_equilibrium_point label_E_shift shift_guide_lines label_Q_shift waste wastelbl tm_arrow toomuch to 1

    pause 0.3

## Too few vaccines
use: supply_demand
with:
    x_label: Flu shots (Q)
    y_label: $ per shot (P)
    demand:
      label: 'D: own protection'
    supply:
      label: S
    equilibrium:
      p_label: ''
      shift_p_label: ''
    demand_shift:
      amount: 0
      label: True benefit
      arrow: vertical
    region_labels: false
sources:
    - id: reichert
      title: The Japanese experience with vaccinating schoolchildren against influenza
      authors: Thomas A. Reichert, Norio Sugaya, David S. Fedson, W. Paul Glezen, Lone Simonsen, Masato Tashiro
      year: 2001
      finding: Vaccinating Japanese schoolchildren prevented an estimated 37,000–49,000 deaths a year, about one death per 420 children vaccinated, mostly among the elderly; an ecological estimate that later work revised down.
      doi: 10.1056/NEJM200103223441204

    text illus "illustrative" #8f887c x 860 y 640 font_size 24 hidden true
    scratch japan top-right cites ["reichert"] work ["Japan, school flu shots:","1 death prevented","per 420 children (estimate)"] font_size 30 hidden true
    region lost x_from.ref equilibrium_point x_to.ref shift_equilibrium_point between ["demand_shift_curve","supply_curve"] style.fill #87a878 hidden true
    text lostlbl "lost" #5d8a4e at.data [55,80] font_size 30 hidden true
    arrow tf_arrow #5d8a4e from.data [60,6] to.data [50,6] hidden true
    text toofew "too few" #5d8a4e at.data [70,6] font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "Too few vaccines"

Unpaid harm gives too much. So what does an unpaid benefit give? Flu shots, with illust (@draw illus@)rative numbers.
    draw axes

B: Deman (@draw demand_curve label_D@)d is what people will pay to protect themselves. Sup (@draw supply_curve label_S@)ply is what it costs to make and give the shot.

B: Left alone, th (@draw equilibrium_point label_E guide_lines label_Qstar@)e market settles where they cross.

But my shot protects more than me, doesn't it? My grandmother, the bus driver, whoever I'd have coughed on.
    highlight label_D

B: It does. When Japan vac (@draw japan_box japan_line_1 japan_line_2 japan_line_3@)cinated its schoolchildren, one study estimated a death prevented for every four hundred and twenty children, mostly among the old.

    erase japan

    draw demand_shift_curve label_D_shift

So a shot's true value sits above what its buyer will pay.
    animate demand_shift.amount 20 duration 3

    draw demand_shift_arrow

B: And true benefi (@draw shift_equilibrium_point label_E_shift shift_guide_lines label_Q_shift@)t meets supply further out. The town wants more shots than people buy for themselves.

Then the g (@draw lost@)ap is shots worth more than th (@draw lostlbl tf_arrow toofew@)ey cost, which nobody buys.

B: Too much of what harms bystanders, too little of what helps them. One cause: the price leaves them out.
    highlight lost effect pulse

Try it: drag the true-benefit curve higher. The more a shot protects others, the further the market falls short.
    explore

    pause 0.5

## Not every side effect
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Pigou would hire you.
    good: You can spot a missing price.
    poor: 'Ask each time: does a price carry it?'
    none: Smoke slips past most people too.
sources:
    - id: currie_walker
      title: 'Traffic Congestion and Infant Health: Evidence from E-ZPass'
      authors: Janet Currie, Reed Walker
      year: 2011
      finding: Electronic toll collection (E-ZPass) cut congestion and exhaust near toll plazas in New Jersey and Pennsylvania; among mothers living within 2 km, prematurity fell by about 6.7–9.2% and low birth weight by about 8.5–11.3%.
      doi: 10.1257/app.3.1.65

    box buyer "House buyer" x 250 y 590 width 210 height 100 icon house font_size 28 hidden true
    box price "price ↑" x 250 y 450 width 210 height 100 font_size 28 hidden true
    box others "Other buyers −" x 130 y 310 width 220 height 100 font_size 28 hidden true
    box sellers "Sellers +" x 380 y 310 width 200 height 100 font_size 28 hidden true
    edge e_bp buyer -> price hidden true
    edge e_po price -> others hidden true
    edge e_ps price -> sellers hidden true
    text lcap "through a price: transfer" #2f6b8f x 250 y 200 font_size 32 hidden true
    box yard "Boatyard" x 750 y 590 width 210 height 100 icon hammer font_size 28 hidden true
    box mia "Mia −" x 750 y 380 width 210 height 100 icon bread font_size 28 hidden true
    edge e_ym yard -> mia #b5482e hidden true
    label e_ym_lab "fumes" right #b5482e attach_to e_ym hidden true
    text rcap "skips the price: waste" #b5482e x 750 y 200 font_size 32 hidden true
    scratch ezpass cites ["currie_walker"] work ["E-ZPass, US toll plazas","less idling →","premature births nearby −7 to 9%"] x 500 y 430 font_size 38 hidden true
    text conf1 "Direction: consensus" x 500 y 470 font_size 52 hidden true
    text conf2 "Size: contested" #b5482e x 500 y 370 font_size 52 hidden true
    cards cases size 1.5 width 900 deck true check each hidden true
        Harm unpaid: Loud party next door, Factory fouls river
        Benefit unpaid: Neighbour's garden, You learn first aid
        Through a price: Fans bid up tickets, Tech firm ups rents
    text closer "Markets see only what has a price" x 500 y 420 font_size 52 hidden true
    text nextq "a tax?" #8f887c x 500 y 320 font_size 36 hidden true

    card title "Not every side effect"

B: Hold on. Whe (@draw buyer e_bp price@)n I buy a house, I push up prices for every other buyer. Isn't that an externality? Shouldn't I pay for it?

It feels like one. But follow  (@draw e_po others@)the money: the other buyer (@draw e_ps sellers@)s lose, and the sellers gain exactly as much.

That's a pecuniary effect: it trav (@draw lcap@)els through a price. One side's loss is the other's gain, and nothing is wasted.

B: Wh (@draw yard e_ym e_ym_lab mia parallel true@)ereas Mia's ruined bread is nobody's gain.

Right. Only effects tha (@draw rcap@)t skip the price make the market waste; economists call  (@highlight mia@)those technological externalities.

So 'it affects others' isn't enou (@fade buyer price others sellers e_bp e_po e_ps lcap to 0.25@)gh. Nearly everything affects others. The test is  (@highlight rcap@)whether a price carries it.

    erase buyer price others sellers e_bp e_po e_ps lcap yard mia e_ym e_ym_lab rcap

How sure are we that unpriced harm is real? W (@draw ezpass_box ezpass_line_1 ezpass_line_2@)hen E-ZPass cut idling at US toll plazas, premature births nearby f (@draw ezpass_line_3@)ell by roughly seven to nine percent.

    erase ezpass

B: That markets overdo harm and underd (@draw conf1@)o help is consensus. How big each gap is, and so (@draw conf2@) what to do about it, is contested.

    erase conf1 conf2

    draw cases

    ask "Which kind of side effect is each? Sort the cards." on cases store f stage own
        intro: Now some new cases to try it on.
        right: All {f.total} right: you can tell a missing price from one that moves.
        wrong: {f} of {f.total} on the first try. Party and river: harm unpaid. Garden and first aid: benefit unpaid. Tickets and rents: through a price.

    erase cases

    text rec1 "through a price: transfer" #2f6b8f x 500 y 480 font_size 40
    text rec2 "skips the price: waste" #b5482e x 500 y 380 font_size 40
    parallel true

    quiz "A homeowner restores the old façade of her house on a historic street. Everyone who walks past enjoys it; nobody pays her. Left alone, the market gives…" voice b
        right: Too few: the passers-by's pleasure is a benefit nobody pays her for, so fewer owners restore than the street is worth.
        wrong: Who enjoys the façade without paying the owner? A benefit nobody pays for gets too little: here, too few.
        * too many restored façades
        + too few restored façades
        * just enough

    erase rec1 rec2

So why does nobody pay for the pollution? Because no p (@draw closer@)rice asks them to. Markets work only as far as their prices see, and whether a tax can make smoke vi (@draw nextq@)sible is the next question.

    pause 0.5

